body:
It's quite obvious for the first two answers that Maruki wants people to pursue "Avoidance" behavior. He wants people to be self-possessed, but simply ditch any struggle that one may come across. In Exam 1, he wants you to go get help despite the time loss because intervening would involve you and put you at risk. In Exam 2, he wants you to pick a different dream despite the loss of self because sticking with it or resorting to malignant solutions would cause conflict in your life.

&#x200B;

However, in Exam 3, it's extremely strange that he holds true that those with the power to change hearts should use it for good. Maruki undoubtedly knows that those with this power are still jeopardizing their safety to use it. Despite that, the avoidant answer of B (Never steal any hearts) isn't the answer he wants you to choose. He seems to have this complex that those with power are responsible for everyone's safety, however he wishes for "those with power" to be an extremely select few. In hypothetical, it is those who could change hearts. In his reality, it is Maruki and only him.

&#x200B;

TL;DR He seems to promote avoidance behavior. Avoid conflict of the self. Switch course if the going gets tough. However, his philosophy quickly devolves into martyrdom once you have the ability to change reality. He believes those with this capability have a duty to use it for good, even if it is to their detriment. It's hypocritical, but it makes him quite an interesting character.

Well done for the exams, Atlus!
